<description>
# The lights of a microscope on the Rotary Switch (demo of the part)

A circuit given in words, the demo of the Rotary Switch part (`docs/ROTARY_SWITCH.md`): 12 V, a **Rotary Switch** with 2 poles and 4 positions feeding two white LEDs, each through 330 Ω.

- Position 1: both LEDs dark (nothing is wired to A1, A3, B1, B2).
- Position 2 (I): LED1, 26.7 mA.
- Position 3 (II): LED2, 26.7 mA.
- Position 4 (III): both, 26.7 mA each.

Pole A's throws A2 and A4 are wired together to LED1, pole B's B3 and B4 to LED2, both commons to the + of the battery. The throws run clockwise as on the deck: A1 to A3 along the top, A4, B1, B2 down the right side, B3 and B4 along the bottom from the right.

Expected: I = (12 − 3.2) / 330 = 26.7 mA through a lit LED. Simulated [SIMULATED]: 26.70 mA. The LED model (3.0 V threshold, 7 Ω) stands in for a 5 mm white LED of 3.0 to 3.4 V.

Warning left: the wire from A2 crosses the label `SW` at the top left of the body.

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.31.0 (commit 0f4f59f+changes)
- Circuit: rotary_switch_lights.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

None reported.
</description>


